{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c2892a3d-4ba3-4bff-9303-20eeebfd930e","step":{"id":"s1","script":"The chain rule helps differentiate a function built by putting one function inside another.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Chain rule: inside, then outside"},{"op":"equation","id":"nested","region":"A2","latex":"y=f(g(x))"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c2892a3d-4ba3-4bff-9303-20eeebfd930e","step":{"id":"s2","script":"A small change in x first changes the inside function, then that change affects the outside function.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"insidechange","region":"left","latex":"\\Delta x\\to \\Delta g"},{"op":"equation","id":"outsidechange","region":"right","latex":"\\Delta g\\to \\Delta f"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What happens first when x changes?","expected_gist":"The inside function changes first."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c2892a3d-4ba3-4bff-9303-20eeebfd930e","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The total rate of change multiplies these two effects: outside sensitivity times inside sensitivity.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"rule","region":"B2","latex":"\\frac{dy}{dx}=f'(g(x))g'(x)"},{"op":"text","id":"order","anchor":{"el":"rule","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"outside derivative × inside derivative"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c2892a3d-4ba3-4bff-9303-20eeebfd930e","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For y equals sine of x squared, differentiate the outside sine, then multiply by the derivative of x squared.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"example","region":"C1","latex":"y=\\sin(x^2)"},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","region":"C2","latex":"y'=\\cos(x^2)\\cdot 2x"}],"checkpoint":null}}
